### Base URL
@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000/api

### ============================================================
### STOREFRONT SIGN-IN  (F-01.02)
###
### The mirror of /admin/auth. Same JWT secret, different `typ`
### claim — so a shopper's token verifies perfectly against an admin
### route and still gets a 403. Signature valid, audience wrong.
###
### Password only for now. The FRS also asks for email OTP, which
### needs an email provider this project has not chosen yet.
### ============================================================


### Register first — public (F-05.01)
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Meera R",
  "email": "meera@example.com",
  "phone": "9876500011",
  "password": "shopper-password"
}


### ============ SIGN IN ============

# @name customerLogin
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "meera@example.com",
  "password": "shopper-password"
}

###
@customerToken = {{customerLogin.response.body.data.token}}
@customerId = {{customerLogin.response.body.data.customer.id}}


### Wrong password — 401 INVALID_CREDENTIALS
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "meera@example.com",
  "password": "not-the-password"
}

### Unknown email — the SAME 401 and the same message.
### Anything else answers "does this person shop here?" for free.
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@example.com",
  "password": "shopper-password"
}


### ============ THE SIGNED-IN SHOPPER ============

### Who am I (F-05.02)
### Re-reads the row rather than trusting the token, so an account
### closed from the admin panel stops working at once.
GET {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

### Refused: 403, an admin token is not a customer token
GET {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Edit my own details (F-05.02 / F-05.06)
### No `:id` in the path — the row edited is the one the token names,
### so there is nothing for a caller to substitute.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "phone": "9876500022"
}

### Refused: 409, and the clashing field is named in `errors`
PUT {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "jane@example.com"
}

### Refused: 400, nothing to change
PUT {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}


### ============ PASSWORD  (F-05.06) ============

### Change my own password.
###
### Three checks, each load-bearing: the token is genuine, it is a
### customer's and not an admin's, and the id is the caller's own.
### The current password is required on top.
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/{{customerId}}/change-password
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "oldPassword": "shopper-password",
  "newPassword": "a-different-password"
}

### Refused: 404 for somebody else's id.
### 404 and not 403 on purpose — "that account exists but is not
### yours" still confirms the account exists.
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/37eaec34-f7b4-4001-95b1-1f2c18535288/change-password
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "oldPassword": "shopper-password",
  "newPassword": "a-different-password"
}


### ============ SIGN OUT ============

### Best-effort. JWTs are stateless, so the client discarding its
### token IS the logout — this exists so the act is auditable and a
### future denylist has somewhere to live.
POST {{baseUrl}}/customers/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}
